  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nAs an upper-level trough moves out of SD today, northwest flow will \nset up over the state and then sticks around into the weekend. High \nsurface pressure will be over the area today and will help to keep \nshowers and storms from developing most of the day. However, a \ncouple of models have hinted at some isolated shower development \nover central and northeastern SD during the late afternoon into the \nevening. There is low confidence in this occurring because of the \ndry air mass over the state, but if there was a bit of moisture, \nthen something might be able to pop up. The northwest flow will help \nto push the high surface pressure out of SD this evening.\n\nSpeaking of drier air, the minimum relative humidity this afternoon \nis expected to drop around and slightly below 20% in some areas in \nnorth central SD. Winds are expected to be fairly light in these \nareas, with occasional gust of 15 to 20 mph possible during the \nafternoon. These lighter winds help to keep fire weather concerns a \nbit lower, however the dry conditions could be a bit of a concern as \nit could make it easier for something to start. Temperatures today \nwill be warming into the upper 70s to mid 80s over central and \nnortheastern SD, with warmer temperatures expected in the coming \ndays. Wildfire smoke will continue to be pushed in aloft over SD \ntoday and into Thursday, which could create some hazy skies in some \nareas. As the surface high pressure moves southeast, some of that \nsmoke might make it to the surface over central SD. It doesn't look \nlike there will be large concentrations of smoke that could reduce \nvisibilities, though individuals who are sensitive might experience \nsome effects from the smoke. \n\nIn the coming days, there does look to be occasional weak upper-\nlevel shortwaves that move over SD along the northwest flow. These \nshortwaves will help to support some surface boundaries moving over \nthe state as well as the development of a low level jet. There could \nbe some scattered showers and storms that develops over central and \nnortheastern SD Thursday, however there is lower confidence in the \ndevelopment, with only a 20-30% chance. This is due to a few \nfactors, such as if there might be some moisture might be moving in, \nwhen the surface boundary would move through central and \nnortheastern SD, and when the low level jet develops. Models \ncontinue to vary the location, timing, and if any precipitation \ndevelops at all. The low level jet could also help something to \ndevelop Friday night as well. The next larger chance, 30-50%, \nfor showers and storms to develop looks to be Sunday evening \ninto Monday morning. The models show more moisture moving in \naloft as well as a frontal boundary moving through central and \nnortheastern SD to help storms develop. There is still \nvariability in the models as to the location and timing of the \nshowers and storms, which lowers confidence slightly. These \nchances for storm and shower development in the coming days will\nneed to be watched to see if the models come to more agreement \nas they get closer.",
